copy of it: `collections.defaultdict([])` gives each new key its own empty list,
and a program that wants a different value for each key writes it with
`collections.put`.

A deque is an opaque record of two linked lists, `front` and `back`, with the
front stored in reverse. `push_left`, `push_right`, `pop_left` and `pop_right`
therefore cost constant time on average; reversing one side into the other when
it empties is linear once and is paid for by the pops that emptied it. A pop
answers `{value, deque}` so the shorter deque is the one to bind; an empty deque
answers `nothing` as the value rather than refusing. This is what a list work
queue does not have: `shift` reads the first item but does not remove it, so the
only removal from the front is `skip(xs, 1)`, which copies the rest of the list
on every call.

A named tuple is an ordinary record whose fields are named at run time:

A counter counts equal values of any kind in one pass and keeps first-appearance
order; `collections.count` answers zero for a key it has never seen.
A defaultdict's factory is a value copied into each missing key, and `get`
answers `{value, dict}` so the updated dictionary is the one to bind. The writer
is `put` because `set` is a keyword. A deque pushes and pops at both ends in
constant time on average and answers `{value, deque}` from a pop. A named tuple
is a record, and `collections.field` refuses a missing name rather than
answering `nothing` the way a record property does.
