"| `src/runtime.rs` | +430 −1 | `collection_pairs`, the Counter/defaultdict/deque helpers, linked deque nodes with one reversal, named-record validation, and the thirteen `collections.*` arms |",
"| `src/runtime.rs` | +560 −1 | `collection_pairs`, the Counter/defaultdict/deque helpers, persistent stack trees for deque ends, named-record validation, and the thirteen `collections.*` arms |",
1)
s = s.replace(
"| `tests/collections_test.rs` | +352 −0 | 18 new tests, all running the program on both engines and comparing values as data as well as text |",
"| `tests/collections_test.rs` | +357 −0 | 18 new tests, all running the program on both engines and comparing values as data as well as text |",
1)
old = '''A deque is `{front, back}` with linked `{value, next}` nodes. Pushing at an empty
end reverses the other side once, so each element is moved a bounded number of
times; popping is constant time. Empty pops answer `nothing`.
'''
new = '''A deque is `{kind: "deque", front, back}`. The two ends are persistent stacks
whose spines hold complete binary trees rather than one record per item, so a
deque of n items nests O(log n) levels instead of n. Pushing at an empty end
moves the other side across once; empty pops answer `nothing`. The `kind` field
marks a record the module made, so a bare record or one missing an end is
refused rather than treated as an empty deque.
'''
assert old in s
s = s.replace(old, new, 1)
start = s.index('### Measured cost of `list.shift`')
end = s.index('## Tests added')
old_section = s[start:end]
new_section = '''### Deque traversal depth

The first implementation stored each end as a chain of `{value, next}` records,
so an n-item deque was n nested records and any recursive consumer could abort
on a large one. The stacks now merge equal-sized trees on push, which keeps both
the spine and every tree logarithmic. The replacement test asserts that a
4,000-item deque nests no more than 64 levels, which is a structural property
rather than a wall-clock measurement. `list.shift` still only reads the first
item; `skip(xs, 1)` is still the spelling that removes it.

'''
s = s[:start] + new_section + s[end:]
s = s.replace(
"| `deque_removal_from_the_front_is_not_quadratic_like_skip` | timing: 4,000-item deque drain versus `skip(xs, 1)` |",
"| `edge_a_large_deque_keeps_its_traversal_depth_bounded` | nesting_recursion: 4,000-item deque nests a bounded number of levels |",
1)
s = s.replace(
"- nesting_recursion — covered by Counter over list and record keys, and by the\n  linked deque traversal; no recursive user callback is invoked.",
"- nesting_recursion — covered by Counter over list and record keys, and by\n  `edge_a_large_deque_keeps_its_traversal_depth_bounded`, which measures the\n  deque value's nesting depth instead of assuming it; no recursive user callback\n  is invoked.",
1)
s = s.replace(
"- resource_limit — covered by the 4,000-item timing test; the deque has no\n  unbounded extra store beyond its elements, and Counter/defaultdict append one\n  entry per distinct key. No new limit is needed because these builtins are one\n  VM step and do not recurse into user code.",
"- resource_limit — covered by the 4,000-item depth test; the deque stores one\n  tree node per item at most, and Counter/defaultdict append one entry per\n  distinct key. No new limit is needed because these builtins are one VM step\n  and do not recurse into user code.",
1)
s = s.replace(
"| `cargo test --all-targets` | TOTAL_PASSED passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored |\n| `./rbops/verify.sh phase-099` | VERIFY_RESULT |",
"| `cargo test --all-targets` | 2354 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored |\n| `./rbops/verify.sh phase-099` | pass |",
1)
old_gap = '''- The timing test measures the tree-walker only. Both engines reach the same
  builtins and the cost under test is in `skip` and the deque representation,
  but a bytecode-specific timing claim is not made.
- `list.shift` is a constant-time read in this tree and does not remove the
  first item; the quadratic spelling is `skip(xs, 1)`. The report records both
  costs rather than assuming the finding's stated shape.
'''
new_gap = '''- `list.shift` is a constant-time read in this tree and does not remove the
  first item; the quadratic spelling is `skip(xs, 1)`.
- A deque's `kind` marker makes an ordinary record fail the module's own shape
  check, but a caller who writes the marker by hand can still construct the
  record shape. The operations validate the fields and tree nodes they read, so
  a forged record is refused when it is malformed rather than trusted.
'''
assert old_gap in s
s = s.replace(old_gap, new_gap, 1)
p.write_text(s)
