    23	| `tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt` | +8 −7 | classifies the new SPEC block and re-anchors the blocks after it |
    24	| `tests/doc_blocks_fragments.txt` | +1 −1 | count 38/107 → 39/108 |
    25	| `tests/doc_blocks_known_fail.txt` | +1 −1 | count 14/107 → 14/108 |
    26	| `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` | +2 −1 | acknowledges the `resume phase-099:` delivery subject |
    27	
    28	Counter stores first-appearance order as `{counts: [[key, count], ...], missing:
    29	0}`. `collections.count` answers the documented `0` for a key it never saw.
    30	`collections.most_common` sorts by descending count and keeps first-appearance
    31	order for equal counts. Grouping reuses the existing `group_value_hash`, so
    32	counting is linear rather than a nested scan.
    33	
    34	`defaultdict` is `{entries: [[key, value], ...], factory: value}`.
    35	`collections.get` appends the factory value on the first read and returns
    36	`{value, dict}`; `collections.put` is the explicit write. The spelling is `put`
    37	because `set` is a reserved word, so `collections.set(...)` is not a legal
    38	property name. The factory is a value, not a callable; that limit is stated in
    39	`SPEC.md` and is a known follow-up below.
    40	
    41	A deque is `{kind: "deque", front, back}`. The two ends are persistent stacks
    42	whose spines hold complete binary trees rather than one record per item, so a
    43	deque of n items nests O(log n) levels instead of n. Pushing at an empty end
    44	moves the other side across once; empty pops answer `nothing`. The `kind` field
    45	marks a record the module made, so a bare record or one missing an end is
    46	refused rather than treated as an empty deque.
    47	
    48	`collections.namedtuple(names, values)` builds an ordinary record, so fields are
    49	read with `.name`. `collections.field(record, name)` is the explicit accessor
    50	that refuses a missing field with a catchable runtime error naming the field,
    51	instead of answering `nothing`.
    52	
    53	### Deque traversal depth
    54	
    55	The first implementation stored each end as a chain of `{value, next}` records,
    56	so an n-item deque was n nested records and any recursive consumer could abort
    57	on a large one. The stacks now merge equal-sized trees on push, which keeps both
    58	the spine and every tree logarithmic. The replacement test asserts that a
    59	4,000-item deque nests no more than 64 levels, which is a structural property
    60	rather than a wall-clock measurement. `list.shift` still only reads the first
    61	item; `skip(xs, 1)` is still the spelling that removes it.
    62	
    63	## Tests added
    64	
    65	| Test | Edge class covered |
    66	|---|---|
    67	| `counter_counts_mixed_value_types_and_reports_missing_as_zero` | mixed key types; literal missing value `0` |
    68	| `most_common_orders_by_count_and_keeps_first_appearance_for_ties` | ordering and stable ties, asserted on a literal list |
    69	| `edge_an_empty_counter_answers_empty_and_zero` | empty collection |
    70	| `counter_counts_unicode_yes_no_nothing_and_records` | Unicode, `yes`, `nothing` and record keys |
    80	| `edge_a_malformed_counter_is_refused` | type mismatch: non-list and malformed counter |
    81	| `edge_a_malformed_defaultdict_is_refused` | type mismatch: malformed defaultdict |
    82	| `edge_a_malformed_deque_is_refused` | type mismatch: malformed deque and non-deque pop |
    83	| `edge_namedtuple_refuses_mismatched_names_values_and_duplicates` | failure: length mismatch, duplicate name, non-text name |
    84	| `edge_a_large_deque_keeps_its_traversal_depth_bounded` | nesting_recursion: 4,000-item deque nests a bounded number of levels |
    85	
    86	Mandatory matrix:
    87	
    88	- empty — covered by `edge_an_empty_counter_answers_empty_and_zero`, the empty
    89	  deque pop and `edge_an_empty_namedtuple_is_an_empty_record`.
    90	- singleton — covered by the one-element deque and named-record cases.
    91	- boundary — covered by popping the last item at each deque end and by
    92	  `most_common`'s equal-count ordering.
    93	- out_of_bounds — N/A: these values expose no numeric index; an empty pop answers
    94	  `nothing` instead of indexing, and a missing named field is refused by name.
    95	- type_mismatch — covered by `edge_a_malformed_counter_is_refused`,
    96	  `edge_a_malformed_defaultdict_is_refused`, `edge_a_malformed_deque_is_refused`
    97	  and the named-record refusals.
    98	- numeric_boundary — N/A: counts are non-negative integers and no arithmetic is
    99	  exposed; there is no float, overflow or NaN input to a collection operation.
   100	- unicode — covered by `counter_counts_unicode_yes_no_nothing_and_records` and
   101	  `edge_a_namedtuple_field_name_may_be_any_text`.
   102	- nesting_recursion — covered by Counter over list and record keys, and by
   103	  `edge_a_large_deque_keeps_its_traversal_depth_bounded`, which measures the
   104	  deque value's nesting depth instead of assuming it; no recursive user callback
   105	  is invoked.
   106	- duplicate_missing_keys — covered by Counter equality for repeated values,
   107	  `most_common` ties, missing Counter reads, `defaultdict.get` creation, and
   108	  duplicate named fields being refused.
   109	- malformed_input — covered by the four malformed-input refusal tests.
   110	- resource_limit — covered by the 4,000-item depth test; the deque stores one
   111	  tree node per item at most, and Counter/defaultdict append one entry per
   112	  distinct key. No new limit is needed because these builtins are one VM step
   113	  and do not recurse into user code.
   114	
   115	## Gates
   116	
   117	| Gate | Result |
   118	|---|---|
   119	| `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | pass |
   120	| `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | pass |
   121	| `cargo test --all-targets` | 2354 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored |
   122	| `./rbops/verify.sh phase-099` | pass |
   123	
   124	## Invariants touched
   125	
   126	- None. `Value`, `Error`, the grammar, `.rb`, `to … end`, `set … to`, `say`,
   127	  trailing commas, interpolation, examples and modules are unchanged.
   128	- `collections` is a module name; the new names are builtins reached by both
   129	  engines through the existing shared dispatcher.
   130	
   131	## Known gaps / follow-ups
   132	
   133	- The `defaultdict` factory is a stored value, not a callable. Invoking a
   134	  Redblue function from a native builtin would need a callback path back into
   135	  the interpreter and the VM; that is a separate concern. `collections.put`
   136	  remains the explicit write, and `SPEC.md` states the value-factory rule.
   137	- `list.shift` is a constant-time read in this tree and does not remove the
   138	  first item; the quadratic spelling is `skip(xs, 1)`.
   139	- A deque's `kind` marker makes an ordinary record fail the module's own shape
   140	  check, but a caller who writes the marker by hand can still construct the
   141	  record shape. The operations validate the fields and tree nodes they read, so
   142	  a forged record is refused when it is malformed rather than trusted.
